T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Ninety emergency surgical patients undergoing open abdominal procedures were randomized to) Tj
T*
(either five days of prophylactic negative pressure wound closure \(n = 45\) or standard) Tj
T*
(sterile dressing management \(n = 45\). Postoperative surgical site infection developed in) Tj
T*
(19 patients \(42.2%\) in the standard dressing group versus 8 patients \(17.8%\) in the) Tj
T*
(negative pressure group \(p = 0.019\). Superficial infection incidence was significantly) Tj
T*
(lower with prophylactic vacuum therapy \(17.8% versus 37.8%, p = 0.032\), while deep) Tj
T*
(infection rates showed no meaningful difference \(4.4% each group, p = 1.0\). Additional) Tj
T*
(benefits included accelerated wound healing progression and improved cosmetic appearance) Tj
T*
(at 30-day assessment. One adverse event \(skin irritation at vacuum interface\) was) Tj
T*
(documented in the negative pressure group. These findings demonstrate that prophylactic) Tj
T*
(negative pressure wound therapy provides meaningful infection reduction without) Tj
T*
(significant safety concerns, supporting its use in high-risk emergency surgical contexts.) Tj
